mlflow|10.4|4.6,8.3,10.3|LLMs and Agents: tracing;Evaluation datasets;Evaluation experiments and scorers;Recording feedback and comparison|Open the LLMs and Agents evaluation documentation during AI and LLM Evaluation in Phase 10 after a working LLM application and testing; do not use the traditional model-registry section for this visit.|MLflow can retain LLM traces, evaluation cases and comparisons; lifecycle/registry work belongs to the later MLOps visit.|Record a small fixed document-assistant or CCAI evaluation with input/source/output provenance and one changed version, inspecting failed or disputed scores.|Distinguish LLM evaluation from traditional model registry;Keep comparison data and scorer settings reproducible|The two versions use identical cases;A failure trace and scorer limitation are documented|4,6|10.5|during_topic
otel|11.1|7.3,9.1,10.8|Trace context propagation;Spans and semantic conventions;Resource attributes;Sampling;Sensitive-data handling|Revisit propagation and instrumentation design during AI System Architecture in Phase 11 after implementing basic observability.|The architecture now spans several boundaries, so an isolated request log is insufficient to explain a slow or failed flow.|Annotate a CCAI request diagram with span boundaries, propagation, safe attributes and sampling decisions; identify where private context must be excluded.|Connect a dependency boundary to a trace;Choose useful low-cardinality attributes|A request can be followed across the proposed boundaries;The plan excludes secrets and raw private document content|6,7|11.2|during_topic
neo4j|8.6|8.1,8.3,8.5|Cypher relationship patterns;Bounded traversals;Properties and source provenance;Indexes and query plans|Read relationship-query and traversal sections during Graph RAG in Phase 08 after a working vector RAG baseline and graph fundamentals.|The graph database implements relationship retrieval; extraction quality and answer grounding must still be evaluated separately.|Retrieve a bounded provenance-bearing subgraph for a relationship question and compare its evidence with vector-only retrieval on the same question set.|Bound graph expansion;Preserve provenance in retrieved relationships|Every returned relationship has a source;The report includes one extraction error and a case where the graph does not help|5|8.7|during_topic
owasp|10.10|3.5,10.1,10.2|Sensitive information disclosure;Data and model poisoning;Unsafe output handling;Unbounded consumption;Risk mitigations|Revisit disclosure and data-handling risks during Privacy, Governance, and Responsible AI in Phase 10 after the security topics.|OWASP supports engineering controls and threat analysis; current legal applicability must come from the official jurisdiction-specific sources.|Review one CCAI data flow for prompt/log disclosure, retention exposure and cross-tenant access, then separate engineering findings from legal questions.|Map an AI risk to a concrete data boundary;Distinguish a security control from proof of compliance|The review names a testable mitigation and its limits;No claim of legal compliance relies only on OWASP|4,6,7|11.1|during_topic
ml-course|5.1|4.2,4.5,4.6|Neural networks module;Activation functions;Backpropagation intuition;Overfitting and regularization|Read the neural-network modules during Deep Learning Fundamentals in Phase 05 after ML evaluation and model training.|The conceptual course connects earlier loss/gradient ideas to hidden layers; the PyTorch resource supplies implementation details later.|Sketch a tiny network's forward computation and explain how a parameter update could reduce loss, then compare two supplied training/validation curves.|Explain the role of an activation;Identify overfitting from separate training and validation evidence|The sketch labels inputs, parameters and output;The curve interpretation proposes a testable regularization change|2,3|5.2|during_topic
pgvector|8.1|2.5,3.2|Getting started: vector type and dimensions;Querying: distance operators;Exact nearest-neighbor search;Storing vectors|Use only the vector type and exact-search sections during Embeddings and Vector Search in Phase 08 after the introductory similarity lessons. Defer ANN indexes until Vector Databases.|A small SQL example connects embedding geometry to a durable query without making index tuning a prerequisite for embeddings.|Store a few supplied same-model vectors and compare exact rankings under cosine and inner-product operators, retaining source IDs.|Keep dimensions and model identity consistent;Interpret a distance operator's ranking|Document vector provenance and dimensions;One expected relevant result is checked manually|4,5|8.2|during_topic
pgvector|8.3|3.2,8.1,8.2|Querying nearest neighbors;Filtering;Joins and source metadata;Index query behavior|Revisit filtering and vector-query sections during Traditional RAG in Phase 08 after an exact/indexed search baseline.|The database must return authorized evidence with provenance; pgvector itself does not generate grounded answers.|Write a tenant-filtered retrieval query that joins chunk/source metadata, then test an allowed query and a denied cross-tenant fixture.|Combine similarity and ownership constraints;Keep retrieved chunks traceable to sources|Another tenant's chunks cannot appear;Returned evidence includes source IDs and the query/model version|4,5|8.4|during_topic
pgvector|8.4|3.2,8.1,8.2,8.3|HNSW and IVFFlat indexes;Filtering and approximate search;Iterative scans where supported;Exact-search comparison;Query plans|Read index/filter tuning sections during Advanced RAG in Phase 08 only after measuring a working RAG baseline.|Approximate search and metadata filters can change recall; source-level measurements must precede a tuning claim.|Compare exact retrieval with one ANN configuration on fixed labeled queries under the same tenant filter; record recall, latency and query settings.|Measure recall against an exact baseline;Explain filter/index interactions|The same authorized cases are used for both runs;The report records index parameters and one failure case|4,5|8.5|during_topic
linux|1.6-bash|0.4,1.5|WSL basic commands;Set up a WSL development environment;Windows/Linux file interoperability;Running Linux commands from Windows|Consult the WSL environment and interoperability sections while studying Bash in Phase 01, after the command-line setup. This is an environment reference, not a Bash-language manual.|You need to know where a shell script runs and how paths cross the Windows/Linux boundary; the Bash lessons supply language concepts.|Run a supplied harmless input-checking script in a temporary WSL directory and document its working directory, interpreter and Windows/Linux path mapping.|Identify the shell environment and file boundary;Distinguish WSL setup from Bash syntax|The supplied script runs in the documented environment;Linux/macOS learners can state the equivalent existing-shell setup without installing WSL|1,7|3.1|later_reference
